.TH AGENTBOX.TOML 5 "2026-08-10" "agentbox 0.0.1-dev" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
agentbox.toml \- agentbox project configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./agentbox.toml
.br
.B ./.agentbox.toml
.br
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentbox/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
agentbox is configured by TOML files merged in four layers, lowest
precedence first:
.PP
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Built-in defaults, compiled into the binary.
.IP 2. 4
User:
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentbox/config.toml .
.IP 3. 4
Workspace:
.B agentbox.toml
or
.B .agentbox.toml
in the workspace root. Having both is an error.
.IP 4. 4
Flags on the command line.
.RE
.PP
There is deliberately no system layer, no environment-variable layer and no
profile layer: each was one more place a value could come from that nobody
thought to look at.
.B agentbox config --origin
attributes every key to the layer that set it.
.PP
Scalars replace. Tables merge key-wise. Arrays append, with two escapes:
.B "!reset"
discards everything inherited for that key, and
.BI ! value
removes an inherited element.
.PP
Validation is total and static. Unknown keys are a hard error (exit 78) on
every machine, as are backend-scoped keys placed outside their scoped table.
A configuration that is only wrong on someone else's laptop is worse than
one that is wrong everywhere.
.SH WHEN THE WORKSPACE LAYER WEAKENS SOMETHING
A workspace configuration is written by whoever wrote the repository, not by
the person about to run an agent inside it, and it outranks the user layer.
agentbox lets it win \(em that is what layering means \(em and then refuses
to be quiet about it.
.PP
Any workspace value that
.I loosens
a ranked security key relative to a lower layer produces a warning on every
invocation, naming the key, both values, and the file responsible. The
ranked keys, loosest value first:
.PP
.RS
.TP
.B security.min_isolation
container, vm
.TP
.B network.mode
open, proxy, off
.TP
.B security.mask_mode
view, auto, filter
.TP
.B security.strip_setuid
false, true
.TP
.B workspace.readonly
false, true
.TP
.B security.container.read_only_root
false, true
.TP
.B security.container.no_new_privileges
false, true
.TP
.B security.vm.guest_root
allow, deny
.RE
.PP
Tightening is silent: a project asking for a stronger box is a project
configuration doing its job. A flag both overrides the workspace value and
clears the warning, because a flag is typed by the person at the prompt.
.PP
.B network.allow
and
.B network.bundles
are not ranked \(em declaring the registries a project needs is the ordinary
case. Use
.B agentbox config --origin
to see where a domain came from.
.SH SCHEMA
.nf
version = 1                      # required; only 1 is supported

[box]
default_command = ["bash", "-l"]
idle_timeout    = "30m"

[workspace]
mount    = "/workspace"
readonly = false

[toolchains]                     # preset toolchains, installed pinned
# node   = "24"
# python = "3.13"
# go     = "1.26.5"

[image]
# ref        = "ghcr.io/me/box:2026-06"   # used verbatim; toolchains ignored
# dockerfile = "Dockerfile"               # built as-is; toolchains ignored
base         = "ubuntu:26.04"
packages     = []
# [image.build_args]

[agents]
install = []                     # e.g. ["claude-code"]; init scaffolds this
channel = "stable"               # stable | latest

[network]
mode    = "proxy"                # off | proxy | open
bundles = []
allow   = []
deny    = []                     # evaluated first, always wins
audit   = true

[network.proxy]
read_timeout    = "1h"
request_timeout = "5m"

[resources]
memory     = "8g"
cpus       = 4
pids       = 4096
tmpfs_size = "4g"
nofile     = 65536

[security]
min_isolation = "container"      # container | vm
# backend     = "container"
strip_setuid  = true
mask_mode     = "auto"           # auto | view | filter

[security.container]
read_only_root    = true
no_new_privileges = true
cap_drop          = ["ALL"]
cap_add           = []
userns            = "auto"       # auto | keep-id | host | off
seccomp           = "default"

[security.vm]
guest_root     = "allow"         # allow | deny
nested_docker  = false
memory_backing = "balloon"       # balloon | prealloc

[masking]
ignore_files   = [".agentignore"]
tmpfs_size     = "1m"
files_readonly = true

[variables]
# FOO = "bar"
[variables.passthrough]
names = []
.fi
.SH KEY NOTES
.SS Choosing the image
Three mutually exclusive strategies, highest precedence first.
.B [image].ref
names a prebuilt image used verbatim; agentbox builds nothing and declared
toolchains are ignored.
.B [image].dockerfile
names a Dockerfile built as-is; a relative path resolves against the
directory of the config file that set it, and the build context holds only
the Dockerfile, so it must be self-contained. Otherwise agentbox generates a
Dockerfile from
.BR [image].base ,
.B [toolchains]
and
.BR [image].packages ,
tagging it by a digest of those features so identical configuration reuses
one image.
.PP
A toolchain value of
.B "none"
excludes a toolchain a lower layer enabled.
.SS security.min_isolation
The weakest tier the project accepts. If no available backend meets it,
agentbox exits 69 naming what is missing rather than running somewhere
weaker.
.B \-\-min\-isolation
may raise the floor but never lower it; lowering requires
.BR \-\-force\-isolation ,
which is recorded and warned about on every subsequent invocation.
.SS security.mask_mode
.B view
materializes masks as mounts when the box is created, so a file matching
later is not masked until the box is recreated.
.B filter
evaluates patterns at lookup time through agentbox's own FUSE share, so a
secret created after the box starts is hidden immediately; it requires the
vm tier,
.BR /dev/fuse ,
and a mount route.
.B auto
resolves to filter where deliverable and otherwise to view
.I with a warning naming the reason.
Requesting filter where it cannot be delivered is a configuration error,
never a silent downgrade.
.SS network.mode
.B off
gives the box no egress path at all.
.B proxy
is default-deny: no route out, and the only path is a squid sidecar
enforcing the allowlist with an audit trail. Under proxy the guest needs no
DNS resolver of its own, which also denies it a DNS side channel.
.B open
grants ordinary network access; the allowlist and audit trail do not apply
and agentbox warns on every invocation.
.SS Backend-scoped keys
Keys under
.B [security.container]
and
.B [security.vm]
are validated statically on every machine, whether or not that backend is in
use. Misplacing one is an error naming where it belongs.
.PP
There is no
.B runtime
or
.B hypervisor
key: the vm tier is Kata via Docker, and offering a choice agentbox cannot
honor is precisely how a box that could never be entered came to exist.
.SS Mask patterns
Patterns use gitignore syntax and are read from
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentbox/agentignore
and every file named in
.BR [masking].ignore_files .
A masked file is unreadable from the box and writes to it never reach the
host; under filter mode it does not appear in directory listings at all.
.PP
.B .git
is deliberately absent from the scaffolded patterns: masking it would break
every tool the agent needs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agentbox (1),
.BR agentbox-security (7)
